-- ============================================================================
-- H1 ASSOCIATE DEEP SEED: Safety Performance Statistics & KPIs, beginner tier.
--
-- Six modules, 26 lessons, 132 questions (15 per module bank plus a 42
-- question final exam). The structure row is GENERATED from the tier's
-- manifest.json rather than hand typed, and the question rows from the
-- committed bank JSONs under tools/course-banks/safetystats/beginner.
--
-- What this tier owns: WHAT THE RATE IS. A rate is a count times a base over
-- exposure hours, and the caller names the base; the engine refuses a missing
-- base and names the field. TRIR, DART and lost time on the 200,000 and the
-- 1,000,000 hour bases; the observed FAR, the one rate with a fixed base; the
-- severity rate, which has no single standard; the API RP 754 process safety
-- event rate with the tier as an input; sum then divide across sites and
-- workforces; the rolling rate, one sum-then-divide rate per complete window;
-- and one report worked end to end. Digest sections 1 to 12, and section 28.
--
-- THE TRAP THE TIER IS BUILT ON. The mean of rates is a different number from
-- the pooled rate, and only the base moves a rate between the headline
-- figures. The Associate capstone grades six rates on stated bases and a
-- rolling window, and grades no interval, no test and no chart.
-- ============================================================================

values ('safetystats', 'beginner', '{"modules":[{"key":"m01-a-rate-is-a-count-over-exposure","title":"A Rate Is a Count over Exposure","lesson_keys":["l01-a-count-over-an-exposure","l02-why-hours-and-never-headcount","l03-the-base-is-required","l04-where-the-osha-base-comes-from","l05-the-refusals-by-name"]},{"key":"m02-one-name-several-numbers","title":"One Name, Several Numbers","lesson_keys":["l01-trir-on-the-osha-base","l02-dart-and-lost-time-cases","l03-the-iogp-million-hour-base","l04-same-letters-a-factor-of-five"]},{"key":"m03-fatal-severity-and-process-safety","title":"Three More Rates: FAR, Severity Rate, PSE Rate","lesson_keys":["l01-the-fatal-accident-rate","l02-why-far-alone-has-a-fixed-base","l03-the-severity-rate-has-no-single-standard","l04-days-per-hour-and-days-per-case","l05-the-process-safety-event-rate"]},{"key":"m04-sum-then-divide","title":"Sum, Then Divide","lesson_keys":["l01-pooling-sites","l02-the-mean-of-rates-is-another-number","l03-a-period-with-no-hours","l04-events-with-no-hours-are-refused"]},{"key":"m05-the-rolling-rate","title":"The Rolling Rate","lesson_keys":["l01-a-trailing-window","l02-one-entry-per-complete-window","l03-a-short-month-and-a-large-monthly-rate","l04-the-iogp-five-year-rule"]},{"key":"m06-one-report-end-to-end","title":"One Report, End to End","lesson_keys":["l01-the-bls-worked-example","l02-the-iogp-published-figures","l03-what-the-basis-block-says","l04-the-capstone-brief"]}]}'::jsonb, 1)
